---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import B from "../data/buckets.json";

const FAMLABEL = { blazevic: "Blažević", prpic: "Prpić", papic: "Papić",
                   zubrinic: "Žubrinić", perpic: "Perpić", kalanj: "Kalanj" };
const byFam = Object.entries(
  B.orphaned.reduce((a, o) => ((a[o.family] = (a[o.family] || 0) + 1), a), {})
).sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1]);
const spec = B.labels.filter((l) => l.specific);
const vague = B.labels.filter((l) => !l.specific);
const pctVague = Math.round(100 * B.vague.agree / (B.vague.agree + B.vague.disagree));
---
<Base title="Labels Where the Father Should Be"
  eyebrow={`${B.buckets.length} sorting buckets, ${B.hanging} published people`}
  dek="This archive found four of these being published as ancestors and removed them, because publishing research scaffolding would be a lie. They were removed from the list of people. Nobody removed them from the list of parents.">

  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">A <strong>sorting bucket</strong> is an entry like
    <span class="mono">Unknown Sorting Working Blažević</span> or
    <span class="mono">Brothers [of Josephus] Žubrinić</span>. No priest wrote those words and
    nobody was ever called them. They are labels somebody typed into the father's slot while
    working through a pile of records — and they are still there, still doing the job of a
    parent.</p>

    <div class="grid two" style="margin-top:1.4rem">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>The scale of it</h3>
        <table class="kv"><tbody>
          <tr><th>Buckets acting as a parent</th><td><strong class="mono">{B.buckets.length}</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><th>Published people hanging off one</th><td><strong class="mono">{B.hanging}</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><th>With <em>no other parent at all</em></th><td><strong class="mono">{B.orphaned.length}</strong></td></tr>
        </tbody></table>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Whose loose ends they are</h3>
        <table class="kv"><tbody>{byFam.map(([f, n]) => (
          <tr><th>{FAMLABEL[f] || f}</th><td><strong class="mono">{n}</strong></td></tr>))}</tbody></table>
        <p class="dek" style="margin-top:.6rem">Only the Žubrinići have ever had their loose ends
        counted. <a href={u("/zubrinic-roots")}>That page</a> was itself wrong by 25 until today,
        for exactly this reason.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>The biggest ones</h2>
    <table class="reg">
      <thead><tr><th>The label</th><th>Family</th><th>Published children</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{B.buckets.slice(0, 12).map((b) => (
        <tr><td class="mono" style="font-size:.88em">{b.name}</td>
          <td class="dim">{FAMLABEL[b.family] || b.family}</td>
          <td class="mono">{b.published}</td></tr>))}</tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>Do the labels encode anything real?</h2>
    <p class="lede">Somebody grouped these children for a reason. <span class="mono">Krivi Put 136
    For Sorting Perpić</span> carries a house number; <span class="mono">1800-1830 Birth — Selce
    Papić (Papa)</span> carries a village, a date range and a
    <a href={u("/name")}>nadimak</a>. The question is whether the reason survives well enough to
    place a person by — and it can be tested, because some of the children under each label have a
    place of their own on record.</p>

    <p><strong>It splits sharply, and on one thing: how specific the label is.</strong></p>

    <div class="grid two" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Labels naming a house number or a date range</h3>
        <p style="font-size:1.6em;margin:.2rem 0"><strong class="mono">{B.specific.agree} agree</strong>
          <span class="dim"> / {B.specific.disagree} disagree</span></p>
        <p class="dek">Every testable child matches. <span class="mono">Krivi Put 136</span> is 5 for 5;
        <span class="mono">1800-1830 Selce</span> is 3 for 3 on place <em>and</em> 3 for 3 on the year
        range. These were written by someone who knew something.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Labels naming only a town</h3>
        <p style="font-size:1.6em;margin:.2rem 0"><strong class="mono">{B.vague.agree} agree</strong>
          <span class="dim"> / {B.vague.disagree} disagree</span></p>
        <p class="dek">Right {pctVague}% of the time — worse than useless, because it looks like
        information. <span class="mono">Senj – Unknown To be sorted Prpic</span> is wrong on every
        one of the four children who can test it.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <table class="reg" style="margin-top:1.6rem">
      <thead><tr><th>Label</th><th>What it claims</th><th>Agree</th><th>Disagree</th><th>Untestable</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{[...spec, ...vague].map((l) => (
        <tr>
          <td class="mono" style="font-size:.86em">{l.name.slice(0, 44)}</td>
          <td class="dim">{[l.village, l.house && "kbr " + l.house,
                            l.range && l.range.join("–")].filter(Boolean).join(" · ")}</td>
          <td class="mono">{l.agree}</td>
          <td class="mono">{l.disagree > 0 ? <strong>{l.disagree}</strong> : l.disagree}</td>
          <td class="mono dim">{l.blank}</td>
        </tr>))}</tbody>
    </table>

    <p style="margin-top:1.4rem"><strong>So: {B.specific.blank + B.vague.blank} published people
    with no place of their own sit under a label that names one — and only {B.specific.blank} of
    those sit under a label worth believing.</strong> The rest cannot be placed this way, and this
    archive will not place them.</p>
    <p class="dek">Which is a result worth having either way. The two specific labels are worth
    taking to a registrar as a question; the vague ones should be treated as what they are, a note
    to self by someone half way through a sorting job.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>What follows</h2>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li><strong>Un-publishing is not removing.</strong> A record hidden from the register goes on
      acting as a parent in every tool that asks whether someone has one. That is how
      <a href={u("/zubrinic-roots")}>the fragment count</a> was wrong by 25 for months.</li>
      <li><strong>Nothing here has been edited.</strong> The buckets remain in the tree exactly as
      they are. <a href={u("/method")}>Method →</a></li>
      <li><strong>Sixty-two of the seventy-four are not Žubrinić.</strong> Thirty Blažević,
      twenty-two Prpić, ten Papić and four Perpić have never been counted as loose ends anywhere on
      this site. They are, and now they are listed.</li>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem"><a class="btn" href={u("/zubrinic-roots")}>The Žubrinić fragments →</a>
      {" "}<a class="btn" href={u("/duplicates")}>The same person, twice →</a>
      {" "}<a class="btn" href={u("/impossible")}>What cannot be true →</a></p>
  </section>
</Base>
